WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.863 --> 00:00:03.603
- [Voiceover] Another season
of America's past time

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.606
is just around the corner.

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:08.009
The infield dirt needs raking,

00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:09.797
the batter's box needs chalking,

00:00:09.797 --> 00:00:11.730
and home plate needs to be,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.702
hey, who's supposed to clean home plate?

00:00:13.702 --> 00:00:16.100
- That would be one of the
umpire’s responsibilities.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.017
Got to make sure the plate's clean.

00:00:23.799 --> 00:00:24.632
- [Voiceover] Look at the...

00:00:24.632 --> 00:00:25.465
- Safe.

00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:27.321
- [Voiceover] Excuse
me, look at all the...

00:00:27.321 --> 00:00:28.154
- He's safe.

00:00:28.154 --> 00:00:28.987
- [Voiceover] Umpires.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:29.820
Thank you.

00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.032
For four weeks every
year, 150 aspiring umpires

00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:37.147
descend unto Ormond Beach, Florida,

00:00:37.147 --> 00:00:38.417
all because of this guy.

00:00:38.417 --> 00:00:39.504
Introduce yourself, Hunter.

00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:41.721
- Sure, my name's Hunter Wendelstedt.

00:00:41.721 --> 00:00:44.286
I'm a major league
baseball umpire number 21

00:00:44.286 --> 00:00:45.937
and I am the owner operator of the

00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:47.250
Wendelstedt Umpire School down here

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.822
in Ormond Beach, Florida.

00:00:49.822 --> 00:00:51.906
- [Voiceover] And student's
come from all over the world.

00:00:51.906 --> 00:00:54.621
- [Hunter] We've got
Taiwan, Dominican Republic,

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:58.073
Puerto Rico, Mexico, Canada,
and Germany represented

00:00:58.073 --> 00:01:00.831
internationally, and I
believe we're coming from

00:01:00.831 --> 00:01:03.274
36 of the 50 states.

00:01:03.274 --> 00:01:04.107
- [Voiceover] But what do students learn

00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:05.487
at umpire school?

00:01:05.487 --> 00:01:08.111
- [Hunter] We're putting
the foundation down.

00:01:08.111 --> 00:01:09.609
- [Voiceover] Students learn
the difference between balls.

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:10.442
- Ball four.

00:01:10.442 --> 00:01:11.275
- [Vocieover] And Strikes.

00:01:11.275 --> 00:01:12.372
(yelling)

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:13.205
- [Voiceover] Foul.

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:14.299
- Foul.

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:15.843
- [Voiceover] Fair.

00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:17.521
Guess there's no call for that one.

00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:18.354
Safe.

00:01:18.354 --> 00:01:19.187
- Safe.

00:01:19.187 --> 00:01:20.267
- [Voiceover] And out.
- He's out.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.732
- [Voiceover] But there's
got to be more to it, right?

00:01:22.732 --> 00:01:25.031
- The most important lesson of umpiring,

00:01:25.031 --> 00:01:26.965
you always have to be
honest with yourself.

00:01:26.965 --> 00:01:29.612
If an umpire makes a
mistake, you need to man up

00:01:29.612 --> 00:01:31.695
and be honest with
yourself, and you fix it

00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:33.945
so it doesn't happen again.

00:01:34.855 --> 00:01:37.253
(clapping)

00:01:37.253 --> 00:01:39.238
- [Voiceover] So after
countless calls and balls,

00:01:39.238 --> 00:01:41.239
walks and talks, these
blues collect their diplomas

00:01:41.239 --> 00:01:43.573
and prepare to say
goodbye to umpire school,

00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:45.176
ready to take their
place behind home plate

00:01:45.176 --> 00:01:47.541
at Yankee Stadium, right Hunter?

00:01:47.541 --> 00:01:49.893
- Best case scenario, seven to nine years

00:01:49.893 --> 00:01:52.118
to get a full time major league job.

00:01:52.118 --> 00:01:54.061
You can go to med school and law school

00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:56.771
in the same time that it takes an umpire

00:01:56.771 --> 00:01:58.590
to reach it to the major leagues.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.173
- [Voiceover] OK, so why
would you do it at all?

00:02:01.173 --> 00:02:02.588
- [Hunter] Whether it's little league

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:04.173
or major league baseball,

00:02:04.173 --> 00:02:07.533
every umpire has to
sacrifice something for their

00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:08.950
love of the game.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:09.874
- [Voiceover] Maybe the
majors won't come knocking

00:02:09.874 --> 00:02:11.626
anytime soon, but thanks to the

00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:13.601
Wendelstedt Umpire School,
these umps are ready

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:15.681
to strike out at any opportunity.

00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:17.607
- I really ain't that
sure I get that metaphor.

00:02:17.607 --> 00:02:21.774
- [Voiceover] Hunter, that
makes perfect sense, come on.

